-- Singapore Penal Code 1871 - Chapter 4, GENERAL EXCEPTIONS (ss 76-95).
--
-- Source: Penal Code 1871, 2020 Revised Edition, SSO "Current version as at
-- 09 Sep 2026" (inputs/PC1871.txt, lines 2534-3014).
--
-- Chapter 4A (private defence, ss 96-106A) is pc-exceptions-private-defence.l4;
-- the roll-up over both, and the s 26G(5) / s 301(2) wiring, is pc-exceptions.l4.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- What a reader must know before reading
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- 1. There is no offence in this Chapter, so there is no `offence under s N`
--    and no `charge under s N` here. s 6 makes every offence "understood
--    subject to" these exceptions without repeating them, and CPC 2010
--    s 123(5) asserts only the conditions that CONSTITUTE the offence, so a
--    charge is never refused because an exception might apply. Each exception
--    is exported as its own BOOLEAN ladder, `exception under s N` f, over its
--    own facts record; the charge sheet at integration reads the offence and
--    the exceptions side by side (notes/PLAN.md §5).
--
-- 2. POLARITY. Evidence Act 1893 s 107 (verified 2026-09-26 against the
--    lawplain statutes corpus, EA1893 s 107): "the burden of proving the
--    existence of circumstances bringing the case within any of the general
--    exceptions in the Penal Code 1871 ... is upon the person, and the court is
--    to presume the absence of such circumstances". The charge generator fills
--    every unknown leaf with FALSE. So every leaf here is phrased so that TRUE
--    is the circumstance that brings the case WITHIN the exception, and no
--    `exception under s N` ladder puts NOT over a leaf: an unknown leaf can
--    only ever withhold an exception, which is exactly s 107's presumption.
--    Where the Code states a condition negatively ("which is not intended to
--    cause death", "did not of his own accord ... place himself"), the leaf is
--    the Code's own negative words. Where a proviso takes a case OUT of an
--    exception ("shall not extend to the intentional causing of death"), the
--    leaf is the proviso's words prefixed "not", and a comment says so. The
--    tests assert, for every exception, that the all-FALSE record gives FALSE.
--    FORK E-1 in notes/exceptions-forks.md.
--
--    The one ladder here that DOES put NOT over leaves is s 90, because s 90
--    is not an exception: it is a definition of "consent" that offence modules
--    read ("without her consent"), where an unknown vitiating circumstance
--    filled FALSE leaves the consent standing and so withholds the charge.
--
-- 3. "In good faith" (s 26B) rides as part of the leaf that uses it; its
--    @desc cites s 26B. The Chapter 2 ladder is the drill-down
--    (`in good faith within section 26B`, pc-general.l4).
--
-- 4. Every export takes one input, `f`, its facts record (notes/PLAN.md §2 -
--    "the single input is always GIVEN f"). notes/PLAN.md §5 writes the
--    exception ladders with `e`; `f` was kept because the charge generator's
--    catalogue records `factsParam: 'f'`.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `pc-domain`
IMPORT `pc-general`

§ `Chapter 4 - General Exceptions`

§§ `s 76 - Act done by person bound, or justified by law`

-- 76. Nothing is an offence which is done by a person who is bound by law to do
-- it or justified by law in doing it.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 76
DECLARE `Bound or Justified by Law Facts` HAS
    `bound by law to do it`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the person who did the act bound by law to do it - would it have been illegal for him to omit it (s 43, "legally bound to do")? For example, a soldier firing on a mob by the order of his superior officer, in conformity with the commands of the law (s 76 illus (a)).
    `justified by law in doing it`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the person justified by law in doing the act? For example, seizing a person in the act of murder under the power the law gives to all persons of apprehending murderers in the act (s 76 illus (b)).

@export Whether an act falls within the general exception in section 76 of the Penal Code 1871 (act done by a person bound, or justified, by law)
GIVEN f IS A `Bound or Justified by Law Facts` @desc Whether the person was bound, or justified, by law in doing the act
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exception under s 76` f IF
        "Nothing is an offence which is done by a person who is"
    ... (    f's `bound by law to do it`
         ..  "or"
         ..  f's `justified by law in doing it` )

§§ `s 77 - Act of judge when acting judicially`

-- 77. Nothing is an offence which is done by a judge when acting judicially in
-- the exercise of any power which is, or which in good faith he believes to be,
-- given to him by law.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 77
DECLARE `Judicial Act Facts` HAS
    `done by a judge`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act done by a judge (s 19 - see `judge within section 19` in pc-general.l4)?
    `acting judicially`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the judge acting judicially when he did it?
    `in the exercise of a power which is given to him by law`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was he exercising a power which is in fact given to him by law?
    `in the exercise of a power which in good faith he believes to be given to him by law`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was he exercising a power which he believed, in good faith (s 26B - with due care and attention), to be given to him by law?

@export Whether an act falls within the general exception in section 77 of the Penal Code 1871 (act of a judge when acting judicially)
GIVEN f IS A `Judicial Act Facts` @desc Who did the act, in what capacity, and under what power
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exception under s 77` f IF
        "Nothing is an offence which is done by"
    ... f's `done by a judge`
    ... "when"
    ... f's `acting judicially`
    ... "in the exercise of any power which is, or which in good faith he believes to be, given to him by law"
    ... (    f's `in the exercise of a power which is given to him by law`
         OR  f's `in the exercise of a power which in good faith he believes to be given to him by law` )

§§ `s 78 - Act done pursuant to the judgment or order of a court of justice`

-- 78. Nothing which is done in pursuance of, or which is warranted by, the
-- judgment or order of a court of justice, if done while the judgment or order
-- remains in force, is an offence, notwithstanding the court may have had no
-- jurisdiction to pass the judgment or order, provided the person doing the act
-- in good faith believes that the court had such jurisdiction.
--
-- The proviso is read as governing the whole section, so the good-faith belief
-- is required whether or not the court in fact had jurisdiction: FORK E-2.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 78
DECLARE `Court Order Facts` HAS
    `done in pursuance of the judgment or order of a court of justice`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act done in pursuance of the judgment or order of a court of justice (s 20)?
    `warranted by the judgment or order of a court of justice`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act warranted by the judgment or order of a court of justice (s 20)?
    `done while the judgment or order remains in force`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it done while that judgment or order remained in force?
    `the person doing the act in good faith believes that the court had such jurisdiction`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person doing the act believe, in good faith (s 26B - with due care and attention), that the court had jurisdiction to pass the judgment or order? Whether the court in fact had jurisdiction does not matter ("notwithstanding the court may have had no jurisdiction").

@export Whether an act falls within the general exception in section 78 of the Penal Code 1871 (act done pursuant to the judgment or order of a court of justice)
GIVEN f IS A `Court Order Facts` @desc The judgment or order, and what the person doing the act believed of the court's jurisdiction
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exception under s 78` f IF
        "Nothing which is done"
    AND (    f's `done in pursuance of the judgment or order of a court of justice`
         OR  f's `warranted by the judgment or order of a court of justice` )
    AND "if"
         ... f's `done while the judgment or order remains in force`
    AND "is an offence, notwithstanding the court may have had no jurisdiction to pass the judgment or order, provided"
         ... f's `the person doing the act in good faith believes that the court had such jurisdiction`

§§ `s 79 - Act done by person by mistake of fact believing himself bound or justified by law`

-- 79.-(1) Unless otherwise provided by written law, nothing is an offence
-- which is done by any person who by reason of a mistake of fact or in
-- ignorance of a fact in good faith believes himself to be bound by law to do it
-- or justified by law in doing it.
--
-- (2) To avoid doubt, where a person alleges a mistake of fact or ignorance of a
-- fact that may negate the fault element of the offence that the person is
-- charged with, the prosecution must prove the fault element in order to
-- establish liability under the offence.
--
-- Explanation 1.-Where the physical and fault elements of an offence are
-- proven, and the accused person proves the defence under subsection (1), the
-- accused person is not guilty of that offence.
--
-- Explanation 2.-Where an alleged mistake of fact or ignorance of a fact may
-- negate the fault element of an offence, the accused person does not need to
-- rely on the defence of mistake. [...] The prosecution must prove the fault
-- element in order to establish liability under the offence.
--
-- Subsection (2) and both Explanations are declaratory: they say that a mistake
-- which negates a fault element is answered by the OFFENCE's ladder (its fault
-- leaf is FALSE, so it is not made out) and not by this exception. Nothing in
-- this module can express that; it is carried by the offence modules' fault
-- leaves. Illustration (f) (the look-alike watch: no theft, for want of
-- dishonesty) is the same point and is therefore not a test here.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 79
DECLARE `Mistake of Fact Facts` HAS
    `no written law provides otherwise`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it the case that no written law provides that this defence is unavailable for the offence ("Unless otherwise provided by written law")? Answer TRUE unless a written law excludes it.
    `by reason of a mistake of fact or in ignorance of a fact`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person act by reason of a mistake of fact, or in ignorance of a fact (not a mistake of law - s 79A)?
    `in good faith believes himself to be bound by law to do it`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Because of that mistake or ignorance, did the person believe, in good faith (s 26B - with due care and attention), that he was bound by law to do the act?
    `in good faith believes himself to be justified by law in doing it`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Because of that mistake or ignorance, did the person believe, in good faith (s 26B - with due care and attention), that he was justified by law in doing the act?

@export Whether an act falls within the general exception in section 79(1) of the Penal Code 1871 (mistake of fact, believing oneself bound or justified by law)
GIVEN f IS A `Mistake of Fact Facts` @desc The mistake, and what the person believed in consequence of it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exception under s 79` f IF
        "(1) Unless otherwise provided by written law,"
    AND f's `no written law provides otherwise`
    AND "nothing is an offence which is done by any person who"
         ... f's `by reason of a mistake of fact or in ignorance of a fact`
    AND (    f's `in good faith believes himself to be bound by law to do it`
         OR  f's `in good faith believes himself to be justified by law in doing it` )

§§ `s 79A - Mistake of law or ignorance of law not defence`

-- 79A.-(1) A person's mistake of law or ignorance of the law is not a defence
-- to a charge for an offence unless it is otherwise provided by written law.
-- (2) To avoid doubt, where a person alleges a mistake of law or ignorance of
-- the law that may negate the fault element of the offence that the person is
-- charged with, the prosecution must prove the fault element in order to
-- establish liability under the offence.
--
-- Not a general exception: s 79A(1) says when a mistake of law is NOT one.
-- (2) is declaratory, as s 79(2) is. The ladder is the one case s 79A(1) leaves
-- open - a written law that makes the mistake a defence.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 79A
DECLARE `Mistake of Law Facts` HAS
    `mistake of law or ignorance of the law`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person act under a mistake of law, or in ignorance of the law?
    `a written law provides that it is a defence`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does some written law provide that such a mistake or ignorance is a defence to this offence?

@export Whether a mistake of law or ignorance of the law is a defence, under section 79A(1) of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN f IS A `Mistake of Law Facts` @desc The mistake of law, and whether any written law makes it a defence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `mistake of law is a defence under s 79A` f IF
        "(1) A person's"
    ... f's `mistake of law or ignorance of the law`
    ... "is not a defence to a charge for an offence unless it is otherwise provided by written law"
    ... f's `a written law provides that it is a defence`

§§ `s 80 - Accident in the doing of a lawful act`

-- 80.-(1) Nothing is an offence which is done by accident or misfortune in the
-- doing of a lawful act in a lawful manner, by lawful means, and with proper
-- care and caution.
-- (2) To avoid doubt, where - (a) a person alleges any act is done by accident
-- or misfortune [...]; and (b) the doing of the act allegedly by accident or
-- misfortune may negate the fault element of the offence that the person is
-- charged with, the prosecution must prove the fault element in order to
-- establish liability under the offence.
-- Explanation.-A lawful act in this section is any act which is not an offence
-- under this Code or any written law and which is not otherwise prohibited by
-- law.
--
-- (2) is declaratory, as s 79(2) is; Illustration (b) (the malfunctioning
-- crane: no s 304A offence, for want of negligence) makes that point and is not
-- a test of this exception.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 80
DECLARE `Accident Facts` HAS
    `done by accident or misfortune`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act done by accident or misfortune?
    `in the doing of a lawful act`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the person doing a lawful act - one which is not an offence under the Penal Code or any written law, and not otherwise prohibited by law (s 80 Explanation)?
    `in a lawful manner`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the lawful act being done in a lawful manner?
    `by lawful means`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it being done by lawful means?
    `with proper care and caution`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it being done with proper care and caution?

@export Whether an act falls within the general exception in section 80(1) of the Penal Code 1871 (accident in the doing of a lawful act)
GIVEN f IS A `Accident Facts` @desc How the act came about, and what the person was doing at the time
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exception under s 80` f IF
        "(1) Nothing is an offence which is done by"
    ... f's `done by accident or misfortune`
    ... f's `in the doing of a lawful act`
    ... f's `in a lawful manner`
    ... f's `by lawful means`
    ... "and"
    ... f's `with proper care and caution`

§§ `s 81 - Act likely to cause harm but done to prevent other harm`

-- 81. Nothing is an offence merely by reason of its being done with the
-- knowledge that it is likely to cause harm, if it be done in good faith for
-- the purpose of preventing or avoiding other harm to person or property.
-- Explanation.-It is a question of fact in such a case whether the harm to be
-- prevented or avoided was of such a nature and so imminent as to justify or
-- excuse the risk of doing the act with the knowledge that it was likely to
-- cause harm.
--
-- The Explanation is encoded as a limb: every Illustration ends "if it be found
-- as a matter of fact that the danger ... was such as to excuse him", so it is
-- a condition of the exception, not a gloss (FORK E-3).
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 81
DECLARE `Necessity Facts` HAS
    `an offence, if at all, merely by reason of its being done with the knowledge that it is likely to cause harm`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the only fault the act carries that it was done KNOWING it was likely to cause harm - not intending that harm? (Illus (a): "without any intention to run down the boat C"; illus (c): "without any intention to cause harm to the crew members".)
    `done in good faith for the purpose of preventing or avoiding other harm to person or property`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act done in good faith (s 26B - with due care and attention) for the purpose of preventing or avoiding other harm to a person or to property?
    `the harm to be prevented or avoided was of such a nature and so imminent as to justify or excuse the risk`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the harm to be prevented or avoided, as a matter of fact, of such a nature and so imminent as to justify or excuse the risk of doing the act knowing it was likely to cause harm (s 81 Explanation)?

@export Whether an act falls within the general exception in section 81 of the Penal Code 1871 (act likely to cause harm, done to prevent other harm)
GIVEN f IS A `Necessity Facts` @desc What the person knew of the harm, what he meant to prevent, and how grave and imminent it was
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exception under s 81` f IF
        "Nothing is an offence"
    ... f's `an offence, if at all, merely by reason of its being done with the knowledge that it is likely to cause harm`
    ... "if it be done"
    ... f's `done in good faith for the purpose of preventing or avoiding other harm to person or property`
    ... "Explanation.-It is a question of fact in such a case whether"
    ... f's `the harm to be prevented or avoided was of such a nature and so imminent as to justify or excuse the risk`

§§ `ss 82 and 83 - Acts of a child`

-- 82. Nothing is an offence which is done by a child below 10 years of age.
--
-- 83. Nothing is an offence which is done by a child of or above 10 years and
-- below 12, who has not attained sufficient maturity of understanding to judge
-- of the nature and consequence of his conduct on that occasion.
--
-- Ages are in completed years at the time of the act. The ladders read BOOLEAN
-- leaves; the helpers below decide them from a number (notes/PLAN.md §3.5).
@export Whether a person of the given age is "below 10 years of age" (s 82 of the Penal Code 1871)
GIVEN years IS A NUMBER @desc The person's age in completed years at the time of the act
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `below 10 years of age` years IF years < 10

@export Whether a person of the given age is "of or above 10 years and below 12" (s 83 of the Penal Code 1871)
GIVEN years IS A NUMBER @desc The person's age in completed years at the time of the act
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `of or above 10 years and below 12 years of age` years IF
    years AT LEAST 10 AND years < 12

@ref Penal Code 1871 ss 82, 83
DECLARE `Child Facts` HAS
    `the child is below 10 years of age`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the person who did the act below 10 years of age at the time (s 82)?
    `the child is of or above 10 years and below 12 years of age`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the person who did the act 10 or 11 years of age at the time (s 83)?
    `has not attained sufficient maturity of understanding to judge of the nature and consequence of his conduct on that occasion`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Had the child NOT yet attained sufficient maturity of understanding to judge the nature and consequence of his conduct on that occasion (s 83)?

@export Whether an act falls within the general exception in section 82 of the Penal Code 1871 (act of a child below 10 years of age)
GIVEN f IS A `Child Facts` @desc The age of the person who did the act, and his maturity of understanding
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exception under s 82` f IF
        "Nothing is an offence which is done by a child"
    ... f's `the child is below 10 years of age`

@export Whether an act falls within the general exception in section 83 of the Penal Code 1871 (act of a child of or above 10 and below 12 years of age without sufficient maturity of understanding)
GIVEN f IS A `Child Facts` @desc The age of the person who did the act, and his maturity of understanding
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exception under s 83` f IF
        "Nothing is an offence which is done by a child"
    ... f's `the child is of or above 10 years and below 12 years of age`
    ... "who"
    ... f's `has not attained sufficient maturity of understanding to judge of the nature and consequence of his conduct on that occasion`

§§ `s 84 - Act of person of unsound mind`

-- 84.-(1) Nothing is an offence which is done by a person who, at the time of
-- doing it, by reason of unsoundness of mind, is -
--   (a) incapable of knowing the nature of the act;
--   (b) incapable of knowing that what he is doing is wrong; or
--   (c) completely deprived of any power to control his actions.
-- (2) Subsection (1)(b) applies only if the person is incapable of knowing that
-- his act -
--   (a) is wrong by the ordinary standards of reasonable and honest persons; and
--   (b) is wrong as contrary to law.
--
-- (2) is encoded INSIDE limb (1)(b): the limb holds only when both of (2)'s
-- incapacities do.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 84
DECLARE `Unsoundness of Mind Facts` HAS
    `at the time of doing it, by reason of unsoundness of mind`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc At the time of doing the act, was the person of unsound mind, and was any incapacity below BY REASON of that unsoundness of mind?
    `incapable of knowing the nature of the act`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the person incapable of knowing the nature of the act (s 84(1)(a))?
    `incapable of knowing that his act is wrong by the ordinary standards of reasonable and honest persons`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the person incapable of knowing that his act was wrong by the ordinary standards of reasonable and honest persons (s 84(2)(a))?
    `incapable of knowing that his act is wrong as contrary to law`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the person incapable of knowing that his act was wrong as contrary to law (s 84(2)(b))? A person who knows the act is contrary to law is not within (1)(b), whatever he believes of its morality (s 84 illus).
    `completely deprived of any power to control his actions`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the person completely deprived of any power to control his actions (s 84(1)(c))?

@export Whether an act falls within the general exception in section 84 of the Penal Code 1871 (act of a person of unsound mind)
GIVEN f IS A `Unsoundness of Mind Facts` @desc The person's state of mind at the time of the act
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exception under s 84` f IF
        "(1) Nothing is an offence which is done by a person who,"
    AND f's `at the time of doing it, by reason of unsoundness of mind`
    AND "is"
         ... (    "(a)"
             ..  f's `incapable of knowing the nature of the act`
             OR  (    "(b), which (2) applies only if the person is incapable of knowing that his act"
                  ... "(a)"
                  ... f's `incapable of knowing that his act is wrong by the ordinary standards of reasonable and honest persons`
                  ... "and (b)"
                  ... f's `incapable of knowing that his act is wrong as contrary to law` )
             OR  "(c)"
             ..  f's `completely deprived of any power to control his actions` )

§§ `s 85 - Intoxication when a defence`

-- 85.-(1) Except as provided in this section and in section 86, intoxication
-- shall not constitute a defence to any criminal charge.
-- (2) Intoxication is a defence to any criminal charge if by reason of the
-- intoxication the person charged, at the time of the act or omission
-- complained of -
--   (a) did not know what he was doing; or
--   (b) did not know that such act or omission was wrong,
-- and the state of intoxication was caused without the knowledge or against the
-- will of the person charged with the offence.
-- (2A) Subsection (2)(b) only applies if the person charged, at the time of the
-- act or omission complained of, did not know that the act or omission -
--   (a) was wrong by the ordinary standards of reasonable and honest persons; and
--   (b) was wrong as contrary to law.
-- (3) Intoxication is a defence to any criminal charge if by reason of the
-- intoxication the person charged was of unsound mind as determined in
-- accordance with section 84.
--
-- (1) is the closure: the ladder below is the whole of when intoxication is a
-- defence. (2A) is encoded inside limb (2)(b), as s 84(2) is inside s 84(1)(b).
-- The trailing "and the state of intoxication was caused without the knowledge
-- or against the will" governs both (a) and (b) - it follows both in the text
-- (FORK E-4).
@ref Penal Code 1871 ss 85, 86(3)
DECLARE `Intoxication Facts` HAS
    `by reason of the intoxication did not know what he was doing`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc By reason of intoxication (which includes a state produced by narcotics or drugs - s 86(3)), did the person charged, at the time of the act or omission, not know what he was doing (s 85(2)(a))?
    `by reason of the intoxication did not know that the act was wrong by the ordinary standards of reasonable and honest persons`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc By reason of intoxication, did he not know that the act or omission was wrong by the ordinary standards of reasonable and honest persons (s 85(2A)(a))?
    `by reason of the intoxication did not know that the act was wrong as contrary to law`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc By reason of intoxication, did he not know that the act or omission was wrong as contrary to law (s 85(2A)(b))?
    `the state of intoxication was caused without his knowledge or against his will`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the state of intoxication caused without his knowledge, or against his will (s 85(2))?
    `by reason of the intoxication was of unsound mind as determined in accordance with section 84`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc By reason of intoxication, was he of unsound mind as determined in accordance with s 84 - see `exception under s 84` for that test (s 85(3))?

@export Whether intoxication is a defence under section 85 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN f IS A `Intoxication Facts` @desc The person's intoxication, how it came about, and what it did to his understanding
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exception under s 85` f IF
        (    "(2) Intoxication is a defence to any criminal charge if by reason of the intoxication the person charged, at the time of the act or omission complained of"
         AND (    "(a)"
              OR  f's `by reason of the intoxication did not know what he was doing`
              OR  (    "(b), which (2A) applies only if he did not know that the act or omission"
                   ... f's `by reason of the intoxication did not know that the act was wrong by the ordinary standards of reasonable and honest persons`
                   ... "and"
                   ... f's `by reason of the intoxication did not know that the act was wrong as contrary to law` ) )
         AND "and"
               ... f's `the state of intoxication was caused without his knowledge or against his will` )
    OR  (    "(3) Intoxication is a defence to any criminal charge if"
         ... f's `by reason of the intoxication was of unsound mind as determined in accordance with section 84` )

§§ `s 86 - Effect of defence of intoxication when established`

-- 86.-(1) Where the defence under section 85 is established, then the accused
-- person must be acquitted, except that if the person was of unsound mind by
-- reason of intoxication, section 84 of this Code and sections 251 and 252 of
-- the Criminal Procedure Code 2010 will apply.
-- (2) Intoxication shall be taken into account for the purpose of determining
-- whether the person charged had formed any intention or had any knowledge or
-- belief, specific or otherwise, in the absence of which he would not be guilty
-- of the offence.
-- (3) For the purposes of this section and section 85 "intoxication" shall be
-- deemed to include a state produced by narcotics or drugs.
--
-- (1) is encoded as an outcome. (2), like s 79(2), says that intoxication is
-- also evidence on the offence's own fault leaves; it is carried there, not
-- here. (3) widens the leaves of `Intoxication Facts` (their @desc says so).
-- Not exported: the charge generator draws BOOLEAN ladders only.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 86(1)
DECLARE `Effect of the defence of intoxication` IS ONE OF
    `the accused person must be acquitted`
    `section 84 of this Code and sections 251 and 252 of the Criminal Procedure Code 2010 apply`
    `section 86(1) has nothing to say, the defence under section 85 not being established`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 86(1)
GIVEN f IS A `Intoxication Facts` @desc The person's intoxication
GIVETH AN `Effect of the defence of intoxication`
DECIDE `the effect under s 86(1)` f IS
    BRANCH
        IF   NOT `exception under s 85` f
        THEN `section 86(1) has nothing to say, the defence under section 85 not being established`
        IF   f's `by reason of the intoxication was of unsound mind as determined in accordance with section 84`
        THEN `section 84 of this Code and sections 251 and 252 of the Criminal Procedure Code 2010 apply`
        OTHERWISE `the accused person must be acquitted`

§§ `ss 87, 88, 89 and 91 - acts done by consent, or for a person's benefit by a guardian's consent`

-- 87. Nothing, which is not intended to cause death or grievous hurt, and which
-- is not known by the doer to be likely to cause death or grievous hurt, is an
-- offence by reason of any harm which it may cause, or be intended by the doer
-- to cause, to any person above 18 years of age, who has given consent, whether
-- express or implied, to suffer that harm; or by reason of any harm which it may
-- be known by the doer to be likely to cause to any such person who has
-- consented to take the risk of that harm.
--
-- 88. Nothing, which is not intended to cause death, is an offence by reason of
-- any harm which it may cause, or be intended by the doer to cause, or be known
-- by the doer to be likely to cause, to any person for whose benefit it is done
-- in good faith, and who has given a consent, whether express or implied, to
-- suffer that harm, or to take the risk of that harm.
--
-- 89. Nothing, which is done in good faith for the benefit of a person below
-- 12 years of age, or of unsound mind, by or by consent, either express or
-- implied, of the guardian or other person having lawful charge of that person,
-- is an offence by reason of any harm which it may cause, or be intended by the
-- doer to cause, or be known by the doer to be likely to cause, to that person:
-- Provided that this exception shall not extend to -
--   (a) the intentional causing of death, or to the attempting to cause death;
--   (b) the doing of anything which the person doing it knows to be likely to
--       cause death for any purpose other than the preventing of death or
--       grievous hurt, or the curing of any grievous disease or infirmity;
--   (c) the voluntary causing of grievous hurt, or to the attempting to cause
--       grievous hurt, unless it be for the purpose of preventing death or
--       grievous hurt, or the curing of any grievous disease or infirmity;
--   (d) the abetment of any offence, to the committing of which offence it
--       would not extend.
--
-- 91. The exceptions in sections 87, 88 and 89 do not extend to acts which are
-- offences independently of any harm which they may cause, or be intended to
-- cause, or be known to be likely to cause, to the person giving the consent, or
-- on whose behalf the consent is given.
--
-- Explanation (printed after s 92).-Mere pecuniary benefit is not benefit
-- within the meaning of sections 88, 89 and 92.
--
-- One record for the four sections, because they read the same atoms of one
-- act. "Consent" is s 90's: it rides as a flat leaf whose @desc names s 90's
-- vitiating circumstances, and `consent within section 90` is its drill-down.
-- s 89's provisos and s 91 are leaves in the NEGATIVE of the Code's words
-- ("not the intentional causing of death"), so that an unknown proviso,
-- filled FALSE, withholds the exception (FORK E-1).
@ref Penal Code 1871 ss 87, 88, 89, 91, and the Explanation after s 92
DECLARE `Consent to Harm Facts` HAS
    `not intended to cause death`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act NOT intended to cause death (ss 87, 88)?
    `not intended to cause grievous hurt`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act NOT intended to cause grievous hurt (s 87; grievous hurt is s 320)?
    `not known by the doer to be likely to cause death or grievous hurt`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the doer NOT know the act to be likely to cause death or grievous hurt (s 87)?
    `the person harmed is above 18 years of age`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the person to whom the harm was caused above 18 years of age (s 87)? Decide it with `above 18 years of age`.
    `has given consent, whether express or implied, to suffer that harm`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Had the person harmed given consent, express or implied, to suffer that harm? A consent given under fear of injury or a misconception of fact that the doer knew of, by a person unable to understand what he consented to, or by a child below 12, is not a consent (s 90 - see `consent within section 90`). For example, agreeing to fence for amusement implies consent to harm caused in fair play (s 87 illus).
    `has consented to take the risk of that harm`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Had the person harmed consented, within s 90, to take the risk of that harm (ss 87, 88)?
    `the harm is harm which the doer knew the act to be likely to cause`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the harm one which the doer knew the act was likely to cause (s 87, second limb - consent to the risk covers only such harm)?
    `done in good faith for the benefit of the person harmed`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act done in good faith (s 26B - with due care and attention) for the benefit of the person harmed (ss 88, 89)? Mere pecuniary benefit is not benefit (Explanation after s 92).
    `the person harmed is below 12 years of age`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the person harmed below 12 years of age (s 89)? Decide it with `below 12 years of age`.
    `the person harmed is of unsound mind`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the person harmed of unsound mind (s 89)?
    `done by, or by the consent of, the guardian or other person having lawful charge of that person`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act done by, or with the express or implied consent of, the guardian or other person having lawful charge of the person harmed (s 89)?
    `not the intentional causing of death, or the attempting to cause death`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the act NOT the intentional causing of death, and NOT an attempt to cause death (s 89 proviso (a))?
    `not the doing of anything which the doer knows to be likely to cause death`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the act NOT one which the doer knows to be likely to cause death (s 89 proviso (b))?
    `not the voluntary causing of grievous hurt, or the attempting to cause grievous hurt`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the act NOT the voluntary causing of grievous hurt, and NOT an attempt to cause grievous hurt (s 89 proviso (c))?
    `for the purpose of preventing death or grievous hurt, or curing a grievous disease or infirmity`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act done for the purpose of preventing death or grievous hurt, or of curing a grievous disease or infirmity (s 89 provisos (b) and (c))?
    `not the abetment of an offence to which the exception would not extend`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the act NOT the abetment of an offence to the committing of which the exception would not extend (s 89 proviso (d))?
    `not an offence independently of any harm to the person giving the consent, or on whose behalf it is given`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the act an offence, if at all, ONLY by reason of harm it may cause, or be intended or known likely to cause, to the person giving the consent or on whose behalf it is given (s 91)? Causing miscarriage is an offence independently of such harm, so answer FALSE for it (s 91 illus).

@export Whether sections 87, 88 and 89 of the Penal Code 1871 can extend to the act at all, under section 91
GIVEN f IS A `Consent to Harm Facts` @desc The act, the harm, and the consent
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `not excluded by s 91` f IF
        "The exceptions in sections 87, 88 and 89 do not extend to acts which are offences independently of any harm which they may cause, or be intended to cause, or be known to be likely to cause, to the person giving the consent, or on whose behalf the consent is given"
    ... f's `not an offence independently of any harm to the person giving the consent, or on whose behalf it is given`

@export Whether an act falls within the general exception in section 87 of the Penal Code 1871 (act not intended and not known to be likely to cause death or grievous hurt, done by consent)
GIVEN f IS A `Consent to Harm Facts` @desc The act, the harm, and the consent
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exception under s 87` f IF
        "Nothing, which is"
    AND f's `not intended to cause death`
    AND f's `not intended to cause grievous hurt`
    AND "and which is"
         ... f's `not known by the doer to be likely to cause death or grievous hurt`
    AND "is an offence by reason of any harm ... to any person"
         ... f's `the person harmed is above 18 years of age`
    AND (    (    "who"
              ... f's `has given consent, whether express or implied, to suffer that harm` )
         OR  (    "or by reason of any harm which it may be known by the doer to be likely to cause"
              ... f's `the harm is harm which the doer knew the act to be likely to cause`
              ... "to any such person who"
              ... f's `has consented to take the risk of that harm` ) )
    AND "s 91"
         ... `not excluded by s 91` f

@export Whether an act falls within the general exception in section 88 of the Penal Code 1871 (act not intended to cause death, done by consent in good faith for a person's benefit)
GIVEN f IS A `Consent to Harm Facts` @desc The act, the harm, and the consent
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exception under s 88` f IF
        "Nothing, which is"
    AND f's `not intended to cause death`
    AND "is an offence by reason of any harm which it may cause, or be intended by the doer to cause, or be known by the doer to be likely to cause, to any person"
         ... f's `done in good faith for the benefit of the person harmed`
    AND "and who has given a consent, whether express or implied,"
         ... (    f's `has given consent, whether express or implied, to suffer that harm`
             ..  "or"
             ..  f's `has consented to take the risk of that harm` )
    AND "s 91"
         ... `not excluded by s 91` f

@export Whether an act falls within the general exception in section 89 of the Penal Code 1871 (act done in good faith for the benefit of a child or a person of unsound mind, by or by consent of the guardian)
GIVEN f IS A `Consent to Harm Facts` @desc The act, the harm, and who consented
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exception under s 89` f IF
        "Nothing, which is"
    AND f's `done in good faith for the benefit of the person harmed`
    AND "a person"
         ... (    f's `the person harmed is below 12 years of age`
             OR  f's `the person harmed is of unsound mind` )
    AND f's `done by, or by the consent of, the guardian or other person having lawful charge of that person`
    AND "Provided that this exception shall not extend to - (a)"
         ... f's `not the intentional causing of death, or the attempting to cause death`
    AND "(b)"
         ... (    f's `not the doing of anything which the doer knows to be likely to cause death`
             OR  f's `for the purpose of preventing death or grievous hurt, or curing a grievous disease or infirmity` )
    AND "(c)"
         ... (    f's `not the voluntary causing of grievous hurt, or the attempting to cause grievous hurt`
             OR  f's `for the purpose of preventing death or grievous hurt, or curing a grievous disease or infirmity` )
    AND "(d)"
         ... f's `not the abetment of an offence to which the exception would not extend`
    AND "s 91"
         ... `not excluded by s 91` f

§§ `s 90 - Consent given under fear or misconception, by person of unsound mind, etc., and by child`

-- 90. A consent is not such a consent as is intended by any section of this
-- Code -
--   (a) if the consent is given by a person -
--         (i) under fear of injury or wrongful restraint to the person or to
--             some other person; or
--         (ii) under a misconception of fact,
--       and the person doing the act knows, or has reason to believe, that the
--       consent was given in consequence of such fear or misconception;
--   (b) if the consent is given by a person who, from unsoundness of mind,
--       mental incapacity, intoxication, or the influence of any drug or other
--       substance, is unable to understand the nature and consequence of that to
--       which he gives his consent; or
--   (c) unless the contrary appears from the context, if the consent is given by
--       a person who is below 12 years of age.
--
-- A DEFINITION, not an exception, so it is named `consent within section 90`
-- (notes/PLAN.md §2) rather than `exception under s 90` (FORK E-5). It is read
-- by every section that says "consent" - here ss 87-89 as a flat leaf, and in
-- the offence modules ("without her consent", s 375 and others) at
-- integration.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 90
DECLARE `Consent Facts` HAS
    `consent was given`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person give consent, express or implied, to the thing in question?
    `given under fear of injury or wrongful restraint to the person or to some other person`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the consent given under fear of injury (s 44), or of wrongful restraint, to the person consenting or to some other person (s 90(a)(i))?
    `given under a misconception of fact`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the consent given under a misconception of fact (s 90(a)(ii))?
    `the person doing the act knows, or has reason to believe, that the consent was given in consequence of such fear or misconception`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person doing the act know, or have reason to believe (s 26), that the consent was given in consequence of that fear or misconception (s 90(a))?
    `given by a person unable to understand the nature and consequence of that to which he gives his consent`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the consent given by a person who, from unsoundness of mind, mental incapacity, intoxication, or the influence of any drug or other substance, was unable to understand the nature and consequence of what he consented to (s 90(b))?
    `given by a person who is below 12 years of age`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the consent given by a person below 12 years of age (s 90(c))? Decide it with `below 12 years of age`.
    `the contrary appears from the context`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the section of the Code in which "consent" is being read show, from its context, that a child's consent is to count (s 90(c), "unless the contrary appears from the context")?

@export Whether a consent is "such a consent as is intended by any section" of the Penal Code 1871, under section 90
GIVEN f IS A `Consent Facts` @desc The consent, and the circumstances in which it was given
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `consent within section 90` f IF
        f's `consent was given`
    AND "A consent is not such a consent as is intended by any section of this Code - (a) if the consent is given by a person"
         ... NOT (    (    f's `given under fear of injury or wrongful restraint to the person or to some other person`
                      OR  f's `given under a misconception of fact` )
                 AND f's `the person doing the act knows, or has reason to believe, that the consent was given in consequence of such fear or misconception` )
    AND "(b)"
         ... NOT f's `given by a person unable to understand the nature and consequence of that to which he gives his consent`
    AND "(c) unless the contrary appears from the context"
         ... NOT (    f's `given by a person who is below 12 years of age`
                 AND NOT f's `the contrary appears from the context` )

§§ `s 92 - Act done in good faith for the benefit of a person without consent`

-- 92. Nothing is an offence by reason of any harm which it may cause to a
-- person for whose benefit it is done in good faith, even without that person's
-- consent, if the circumstances are such that it is impossible for that person
-- to signify consent, or if that person is incapable of giving consent, and has
-- no guardian or other person in lawful charge of him from whom it is possible
-- to obtain consent in time for the thing to be done with benefit:
-- Provided that this exception shall not extend to -
--   (a) the intentional causing of death, or to the attempting to cause death;
--   (b) the doing of anything which the person doing it knows to be likely to
--       cause death, for any purpose other than the preventing of death or
--       grievous hurt, or the curing of any grievous disease or infirmity;
--   (c) the voluntary causing of hurt, or to the attempting to cause hurt, for
--       any purpose other than the preventing of death or hurt;
--   (d) the abetment of any offence, to the committing of which offence it
--       would not extend.
-- Explanation.-Mere pecuniary benefit is not benefit within the meaning of
-- sections 88, 89 and 92.
--
-- "and has no guardian ..." is read with the SECOND limb only: its subject is
-- "that person [who] is incapable of giving consent", and illus (a) (the
-- insensible rider, for whom it is impossible to signify consent) says nothing
-- of a guardian, while illus (c) (no time to apply to the child's guardian) is
-- the incapable limb: FORK E-6.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 92
DECLARE `Benefit Without Consent Facts` HAS
    `an offence, if at all, only by reason of harm which it may cause to that person`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the act an offence, if at all, only by reason of harm it may cause to the person for whose benefit it was done?
    `done in good faith for the benefit of that person`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act done in good faith (s 26B) for the benefit of that person? Mere pecuniary benefit is not benefit (s 92 Explanation).
    `the circumstances are such that it is impossible for that person to signify consent`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the circumstances such that it was impossible for that person to signify consent - for example, being insensible (s 92 illus (a))?
    `that person is incapable of giving consent`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was that person incapable of giving consent - for example, a child (s 92 illus (c), (d))?
    `has no guardian or other person in lawful charge of him from whom it is possible to obtain consent in time for the thing to be done with benefit`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Where the person is incapable of giving consent: was there no guardian or other person in lawful charge of him from whom consent could be obtained in time for the thing to be done with benefit?
    `not the intentional causing of death, or the attempting to cause death`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the act NOT the intentional causing of death, and NOT an attempt to cause death (proviso (a))?
    `not the doing of anything which the doer knows to be likely to cause death`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the act NOT one which the doer knows to be likely to cause death (proviso (b))?
    `for the purpose of preventing death or grievous hurt, or curing a grievous disease or infirmity`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act done for the purpose of preventing death or grievous hurt, or of curing a grievous disease or infirmity (proviso (b))?
    `not the voluntary causing of hurt, or the attempting to cause hurt`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the act NOT the voluntary causing of hurt (s 321), and NOT an attempt to cause hurt (proviso (c))?
    `for the purpose of preventing death or hurt`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act done for the purpose of preventing death or hurt (proviso (c))?
    `not the abetment of an offence to which the exception would not extend`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the act NOT the abetment of an offence to the committing of which the exception would not extend (proviso (d))?

@export Whether an act falls within the general exception in section 92 of the Penal Code 1871 (act done in good faith for the benefit of a person without consent)
GIVEN f IS A `Benefit Without Consent Facts` @desc The act, the benefit intended, and why consent could not be had
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exception under s 92` f IF
        "Nothing is an offence"
    AND f's `an offence, if at all, only by reason of harm which it may cause to that person`
    AND "for whose benefit it is"
         ... f's `done in good faith for the benefit of that person`
    AND "even without that person's consent, if"
         ... (    f's `the circumstances are such that it is impossible for that person to signify consent`
              OR  (    "or if"
                   AND f's `that person is incapable of giving consent`
                   AND "and"
                   AND f's `has no guardian or other person in lawful charge of him from whom it is possible to obtain consent in time for the thing to be done with benefit` ) )
    AND "Provided that this exception shall not extend to - (a)"
         ... f's `not the intentional causing of death, or the attempting to cause death`
    AND "(b)"
         ... (    f's `not the doing of anything which the doer knows to be likely to cause death`
             OR  f's `for the purpose of preventing death or grievous hurt, or curing a grievous disease or infirmity` )
    AND "(c)"
         ... (    f's `not the voluntary causing of hurt, or the attempting to cause hurt`
             OR  f's `for the purpose of preventing death or hurt` )
    AND "(d)"
         ... f's `not the abetment of an offence to which the exception would not extend`

§§ `s 93 - Communication made in good faith`

-- 93. No communication made in good faith is an offence by reason of any harm
-- to the person to whom it is made, if it is made for the benefit of that
-- person.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 93
DECLARE `Communication Facts` HAS
    `an offence, if at all, only by reason of harm to the person to whom the communication is made`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the communication an offence, if at all, only by reason of harm it causes to the person to whom it is made?
    `the communication is made in good faith`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the communication made in good faith (s 26B - with due care and attention)?
    `made for the benefit of that person`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it made for the benefit of the person to whom it was made?

@export Whether a communication falls within the general exception in section 93 of the Penal Code 1871 (communication made in good faith)
GIVEN f IS A `Communication Facts` @desc The communication, to whom it was made, and why
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exception under s 93` f IF
        "No communication"
    ... f's `the communication is made in good faith`
    ... "is an offence"
    ... f's `an offence, if at all, only by reason of harm to the person to whom the communication is made`
    ... "if it is"
    ... f's `made for the benefit of that person`

§§ `s 94 - Act to which a person is compelled by threats`

-- 94. Except murder and offences against the State punishable with death,
-- nothing is an offence which is done by a person who is compelled to do it by
-- threats, which, at the time of doing it, reasonably cause the apprehension
-- that instant death to that person or any other person will otherwise be the
-- consequence:
-- Provided that the person doing the act did not of his own accord, or from a
-- reasonable apprehension of harm to himself short of instant death, place
-- himself in the situation by which he became subject to such constraint.
-- Explanation 1.-A person who, of his own accord, or by reason of a threat of
-- being beaten, joins gang-robbers knowing their character, is not entitled to
-- the benefit of this exception on the ground of his having been compelled by
-- his associates to do anything that is an offence by law.
-- Explanation 2.-A person seized by gang-robbers, and forced by threat of
-- instant death to do a thing which is an offence by law - for example, a smith
-- compelled to take his tools and to force the door of a house for the
-- gang-robbers to enter and plunder it - is entitled to the benefit of this
-- exception.
--
-- The Explanations apply the proviso; they ride in its leaf's @desc and are
-- tested as Illustrations.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 94
DECLARE `Compulsion by Threats Facts` HAS
    `the offence is not murder`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence the act would otherwise be something other than murder (s 300)?
    `the offence is not an offence against the State punishable with death`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it something other than an offence against the State (Chapter 6) punishable with death?
    `compelled to do it by threats`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the person compelled to do the act by threats?
    `the threats, at the time of doing it, reasonably cause the apprehension that instant death to that person or any other person will otherwise be the consequence`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc At the time of the act, did the threats reasonably cause him to apprehend that instant death - to himself or to anyone else - would otherwise follow?
    `did not of his own accord, or from a reasonable apprehension of harm to himself short of instant death, place himself in the situation by which he became subject to such constraint`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it the case that he did NOT, of his own accord or from a reasonable apprehension of harm short of instant death, put himself in the situation that subjected him to the constraint? Answer FALSE for one who joined gang-robbers knowing their character, of his own accord or under a threat of being beaten (Explanation 1); TRUE for one seized by gang-robbers and forced by threat of instant death (Explanation 2).

@export Whether an act falls within the general exception in section 94 of the Penal Code 1871 (act to which a person is compelled by threats)
GIVEN f IS A `Compulsion by Threats Facts` @desc The offence, the threats, and how the person came to be under them
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exception under s 94` f IF
        "Except"
    AND f's `the offence is not murder`
    AND "and"
         ... f's `the offence is not an offence against the State punishable with death`
    AND "nothing is an offence which is done by a person who is"
         ... f's `compelled to do it by threats`
    AND "which"
         ... f's `the threats, at the time of doing it, reasonably cause the apprehension that instant death to that person or any other person will otherwise be the consequence`
    AND "Provided that the person doing the act"
         ... f's `did not of his own accord, or from a reasonable apprehension of harm to himself short of instant death, place himself in the situation by which he became subject to such constraint`

§§ `s 95 - Act causing slight harm`

-- 95. Nothing is an offence by reason that it causes, or that it is intended to
-- cause, or that it is known to be likely to cause, any harm, if that harm is so
-- slight that no person of ordinary sense and temper would complain of such
-- harm.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 95
DECLARE `Slight Harm Facts` HAS
    `an offence, if at all, only by reason that it causes, or is intended or known to be likely to cause, that harm`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the act an offence, if at all, only because of the harm it causes, or was intended or known to be likely to cause?
    `that harm is so slight that no person of ordinary sense and temper would complain of such harm`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is that harm so slight that no person of ordinary sense and temper would complain of it?

@export Whether an act falls within the general exception in section 95 of the Penal Code 1871 (act causing slight harm)
GIVEN f IS A `Slight Harm Facts` @desc The harm, and how slight it is
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exception under s 95` f IF
        "Nothing is an offence"
    ... f's `an offence, if at all, only by reason that it causes, or is intended or known to be likely to cause, that harm`
    ... "if"
    ... f's `that harm is so slight that no person of ordinary sense and temper would complain of such harm`
